WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.233 --> 00:00:02.669
Now it's
time to start setting up your evals.

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:06.072
In this lesson, you'll focus on evaluating
each of your agent's skills,

3
00:00:06.373 --> 00:00:07.640
as well as the router's ability

4
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.644
to choose the correct tool and execute
it correctly given a user's request.

5
00:00:11.945 --> 00:00:14.647
You will learn about three types
of evaluators: code based

6
00:00:14.647 --> 00:00:17.717
evaluators,
LLM as a judge, and human annotations.

7
00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.687
You will then apply the appropriate
evaluators to your agent

8
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:23.390
example. Let's get to it.

9
00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.393
There are three main techniques
used for running evaluations.

10
00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:28.528
The first is code-based evals.

11
00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.797
Second is LLMs and judge evaluations.

12
00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:33.433
And finally, human annotations.

13
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:36.503
Each of these techniques
can be applied to agents to measure

14
00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:38.371
and evaluate
different parts of your agent,

15
00:00:38.371 --> 00:00:41.441
but they can also be applied to other LLM
based applications.

16
00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:44.077
So each of these techniques
works for both agents

17
00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:47.147
and your more traditional LLM
applications as well.

18
00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:51.117
Code-base evaluations
are the simplest kind of evaluations

19
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:54.587
when it comes to LLM or agent evals,
and are the most similar

20
00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.891
to traditional software
integration tests or evaluations.

21
00:00:58.458 --> 00:00:59.392
Code-based evaluations

22
00:00:59.392 --> 00:01:02.629
involve running some sort of code
on the outputs of your application.

23
00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:06.533
Some common
examples of these are checking to see

24
00:01:06.533 --> 00:01:08.835
if your output matches a certain regex.

25
00:01:08.835 --> 00:01:11.838
So maybe you want a response
to only contain numbers

26
00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:13.940
or no alphanumeric characters.

27
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.811
You can use a regex to check to make sure
that your response matches that filter.

28
00:01:18.311 --> 00:01:21.214
You might also want to make sure
that your response is Json passable.

29
00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:23.883
or a very common
one that you see, especially in chatbots,

30
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:27.487
is checking to see if the chatbots
response contains certain keywords.

31
00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.324
Like most often, companies don't want
their chatbots to mention a competitor,

32
00:01:31.324 --> 00:01:33.059
so you can do a code-based eval

33
00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:36.296
to see if that competitor name is ever
in one of the agent responses.

34
00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:41.301
But perhaps, maybe the most common type
of code-based eval is to use these evals

35
00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:44.571
to compare your applications outputs
to expected outputs.

36
00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:48.975
So if you have ground truth data of what
the expected output is for an input,

37
00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:52.946
then it's very effective
to use code-based evals to either directly

38
00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.949
compare the output of your application
to that expected output,

39
00:01:56.216 --> 00:02:00.053
or to use something like cosine similarity
or cosine distance

40
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.356
to do more of a semantic match
between those two different values.

41
00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:05.859
The next technique for

42
00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:08.862
running evaluations is called
LLM as a judge.

43
00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:11.297
LLM is a judge,
as the name implies, involves

44
00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:14.634
using a separate LLM to measure the outputs
of your application.

45
00:02:15.602 --> 00:02:18.404
Typically,
the way that it's done is by grabbing

46
00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:23.076
the input and output of your application,
as well as potentially a few other key

47
00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:26.079
pieces of information
from one run of your application.

48
00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:29.249
Constructing a separate prompt
that's going to evaluate

49
00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:32.785
a specific criteria
based on those inputs and outputs.

50
00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.190
Sending that prompt over
to a separate judge or evaluator LLM,

51
00:02:37.924 --> 00:02:40.660
and then having that a LLM, assign a label

52
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.663
on a specific access to that response.

53
00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:45.798
For one more example of how that works.

54
00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:49.302
You can examine this case
of evaluating the relevance of a document

55
00:02:49.302 --> 00:02:51.037
that was retrieved in a RAG system.

56
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:53.239
The retrieval

57
00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:56.242
span in this case
is made up of a user query.

58
00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:59.479
And then the documents
that were retrieved for that given query.

59
00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:01.347
That query

60
00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:03.249
and then the reference documents are then

61
00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:06.452
sent into the separate,
eval template that you see here

62
00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.123
and then populated
so as to ask another LLM,

63
00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:15.295
"Are these reference retrieved documents
relevant to the user's question?"

64
00:03:16.029 --> 00:03:18.765
And then that separate
LLM will either say those documents

65
00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:21.768
are relevant or irrelevant.

66
00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:25.071
An LLM as-a-Judge is really powerful
because you're able to run

67
00:03:25.071 --> 00:03:29.409
large-scale evaluations across
both quantitative and qualitative metrics.

68
00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:33.413
However, it's important to keep a few
things in mind when using LLM as a judge.

69
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.183
The first is
that only really the best models

70
00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:39.819
actually
align closely with human judgment.

71
00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:44.424
So if you're going to run LLM as a judge,
you often need to use a GPT-4o

72
00:03:44.724 --> 00:03:48.328
or Claude3.5 Sonnet
or similarly high-end model to do so.

73
00:03:49.429 --> 00:03:49.929
Even with

74
00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:53.132
these, though, a LLM as a judge
is never going to be 100% accurate.

75
00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:56.836
There will always be some margin of error,
because at the end of the day,

76
00:03:56.836 --> 00:03:59.839
you are using an LLM
to assign that particular label.

77
00:04:00.707 --> 00:04:04.644
You can mitigate some of this by tuning
either your LLM judge prompt

78
00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:06.779
or even your LLM judge model,

79
00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:09.782
and you'll learn a little bit more about
these in later modules in this course.

80
00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.853
Finally, it's always important to remember
to use discrete classification labels

81
00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:17.991
as opposed to undefined scores when
setting up the outputs of your LLM judge.

82
00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:22.462
So you should always use things like
correct versus incorrect, relevant versus

83
00:04:22.462 --> 00:04:27.462
irrelevant, and never a measure like "score
this response on a scale from 1 to 100."

84
00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:31.304
The reason for that is that LLMs
don't have a great sense of what

85
00:04:31.304 --> 00:04:35.174
constitutes an 83 out of 100 versus
a 79 out of 100,

86
00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:38.678
especially when they're evaluating
each case independently.

87
00:04:39.078 --> 00:04:42.015
So always use discrete
classification labels wherever you can.

88
00:04:43.016 --> 00:04:44.217
The third evaluation technique

89
00:04:44.217 --> 00:04:47.520
that you can use
is using annotations or human labels.

90
00:04:48.554 --> 00:04:52.959
The idea here is that you can use tools
like Phoenix or other observability

91
00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:56.963
platforms to construct a queue of lots
of runs of your application

92
00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:01.134
to construct an annotation queue,
and then have human labelers

93
00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:04.337
work their way
through that queue and attach feedback,

94
00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:07.106
or judge
the responses of your application.

95
00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:10.143
The other method that you can use
is actually gathering feedback

96
00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.177
from your end users.

97
00:05:11.177 --> 00:05:14.681
So you might have seen cases in the wild
where LLM systems have a thumbs

98
00:05:14.681 --> 00:05:18.518
up, thumbs down response system
where you can rate the responses of that

99
00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:20.119
LLM system.

100
00:05:20.119 --> 00:05:22.955
And you can use the same technique
inside your application to gather

101
00:05:22.955 --> 00:05:26.125
some feedback or evaluation metrics
about how your app is performing.

102
00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:28.828
When you
have these few different techniques

103
00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:31.831
it can be tricky to decide
which one to use for a given evaluation.

104
00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:35.635
One way to think about
this is how qualitative versus

105
00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:38.938
quantifiable or quantitative is the metric
that you're trying to measure.

106
00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:42.842
If you have something like evaluating
the quality of a summarization

107
00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:46.746
or evaluating the clarity of analysis,
that's a very hard

108
00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:51.417
metric to assign, quantitative
or code-based measurement to.

109
00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:54.620
So that's where you might want to rely on
LLM as a judge

110
00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.690
or human labels
to understand that qualitative metric.

111
00:05:58.191 --> 00:06:01.527
If you have something that's a little bit
more flexible or can be defined in code,

112
00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:04.163
like whether an output matches
a certain regex,

113
00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:07.166
then a code-based evaluation will work
in that case.

114
00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:10.503
The other way to think about these
is whether the evaluation needs

115
00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:15.408
to be 100% accurate, or if it can afford
to be less than 100% accurate.

116
00:06:16.042 --> 00:06:18.578
If you remember, LLM
as a judge is never going to be 100%

117
00:06:18.578 --> 00:06:21.547
accurate
or 100% deterministic kind of technique.

118
00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:25.485
So if you need 100% accuracy,
then you'll need to rely on either

119
00:06:25.485 --> 00:06:27.153
human labels or code-based evals.

120
00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:28.888
What you might

121
00:06:28.888 --> 00:06:32.825
notice here is that human labels
is sort of the best version of evaluation.

122
00:06:32.825 --> 00:06:35.628
In this case, it's
flexible and it's deterministic.

123
00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:38.765
However, in practice it can be hard
to get human labels at scale

124
00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:40.933
because it's a very labor
intensive process to do

125
00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:42.635
lots of labeling on your data.

126
00:06:42.635 --> 00:06:45.638
And if you rely on end users to provide
feedback,

127
00:06:45.772 --> 00:06:49.509
there is some selection bias there over
who chooses to supply

128
00:06:49.509 --> 00:06:51.577
feedback to your application.
So it's generally

129
00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:54.547
not advisable to use that as a large scale
evaluation technique.

130
00:06:55.715 --> 00:06:57.683
Now that you understand
that the techniques that you can use

131
00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:01.888
to evaluate an agent or really
an LLM application, now you'll learn

132
00:07:01.888 --> 00:07:05.158
about the different pieces of your agent
that you can apply these techniques to

133
00:07:05.191 --> 00:07:07.560
to run these evaluations. This lesson

134
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.263
we'll go through evaluating the router
and the skills.

135
00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:11.330
And you'll have a future lesson

136
00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.333
in this course that goes into evaluating
the path in detail.

137
00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:16.335
Starting off with a router.

138
00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:19.338
Routers are typically evaluated
in two different ways.

139
00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:23.176
First, you'll evaluate the function
calling choice, aka

140
00:07:23.176 --> 00:07:26.179
did the router make the right choice
and choosing a function to call.

141
00:07:26.746 --> 00:07:30.383
And if you're using an NLP-based classifier
as opposed to an LLM with function

142
00:07:30.383 --> 00:07:33.619
calling, you can still evaluate
the function calling choice.

143
00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:36.055
And you still use this
sort of evaluation metric.

144
00:07:37.089 --> 00:07:38.090
The second kind of thing that

145
00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.060
gets evaluated in routers is typically
the parameter extraction.

146
00:07:41.327 --> 00:07:44.330
So once the router chooses
which function to call,

147
00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.600
does it extract the right parameters
from the question to pass those

148
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.603
into whatever function
it's decided to call.

149
00:07:50.636 --> 00:07:53.906
One way to evaluate
a router is by using an LLM as-a-Judge.

150
00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:57.710
This is an example of the prompt,
the template that you would use

151
00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:01.080
for your LLM as a judge
to evaluate a router in this case.

152
00:08:01.314 --> 00:08:05.084
So you'll notice at the top
you have some instructions

153
00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:08.087
for the LLM judge
to say what it's going to be evaluating.

154
00:08:08.421 --> 00:08:12.024
You have some places where data will be
added into the prompt, in this case

155
00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:15.027
the user's question,
and then the tool call that was chosen.

156
00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:17.797
And then you have some instructions
saying the LLM judge

157
00:08:17.797 --> 00:08:20.800
should respond with a single word,
either correct or incorrect.

158
00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.135
And then you have some more details on

159
00:08:23.135 --> 00:08:26.138
what incorrect means
and what correct means in this case.

160
00:08:26.572 --> 00:08:29.141
And finally,
you have some tool definitions information

161
00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:32.845
so that the LLM judge knows
all of the possible options

162
00:08:33.145 --> 00:08:35.515
that your application had
when choosing which tool to call.

163
00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:39.719
Looking at the example here,
you might have a case where a user asks

164
00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:42.788
your agent, can you help me
check on the status of my order?

165
00:08:43.089 --> 00:08:45.191
Number 1234.

166
00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:47.994
And the agent says
"definitely" makes a tool call.

167
00:08:47.994 --> 00:08:50.997
In this case, choosing to call an order
status check method

168
00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:54.767
and then extracts that one two, three,
four and says this is an order number.

169
00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:58.704
So it passes it through as an order number
to that order status check method.

170
00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:00.573
And this all looks good.

171
00:09:00.573 --> 00:09:03.409
And it gets back a response
saying the order has been shipped.

172
00:09:03.409 --> 00:09:06.412
It goes back to
the user says your order has been shipped,

173
00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:09.549
and the user follows up and says
"okay, when will arrive?"

174
00:09:09.749 --> 00:09:12.285
And the agent decides
to make another call here,

175
00:09:12.285 --> 00:09:14.954
this time using the shipping and status
check method.

176
00:09:14.954 --> 00:09:18.024
That all looks good so far,
and then it takes that same one,

177
00:09:18.024 --> 00:09:21.127
two three, four and this time
as it has a shipping tracking ID

178
00:09:22.228 --> 00:09:25.231
in this case that's an order number
and not a shipping tracking ID.

179
00:09:25.298 --> 00:09:28.834
So here the agent actually failed
in the parameter extraction task,

180
00:09:29.135 --> 00:09:32.371
even though it succeeded in the function
calling task that it had.

181
00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:36.809
Now, when it comes to evaluating skills,
you can use a few different techniques

182
00:09:36.809 --> 00:09:40.646
to evaluate skills either
standard LLM-based evaluations

183
00:09:40.646 --> 00:09:43.716
or LLM as-a-Judge evaluations
or code-base evals.

184
00:09:44.417 --> 00:09:48.654
And one important thing to call out here
is that skills themselves are really

185
00:09:48.654 --> 00:09:53.092
either just other LLM applications
that have been composed into a skill.

186
00:09:53.092 --> 00:09:57.430
The agent can use, or other software
applications like API calls

187
00:09:57.430 --> 00:10:00.466
or application code that have again
been compressed into a skill.

188
00:10:01.167 --> 00:10:04.637
So all the techniques that you can use to
evaluate skills are really

189
00:10:04.637 --> 00:10:06.606
just the same techniques
that you can use to evaluate

190
00:10:06.606 --> 00:10:10.076
either standard software
applications or LLM applications.

191
00:10:10.676 --> 00:10:13.879
So you might use an LLM as-a-Judge
to evaluate things like relevance,

192
00:10:14.246 --> 00:10:17.216
hallucination, question
answer correctness,

193
00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:20.252
generated code
readability, or summarization.

194
00:10:20.353 --> 00:10:25.353
And then you might use code
based evaluators to evaluate regex matches

195
00:10:25.391 --> 00:10:29.395
Json parse ability in your response
or comparison against ground truth.

196
00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:33.399
Looking at your example agent,
you have three different skills

197
00:10:33.399 --> 00:10:36.569
here. You have a database
lookup skill, a data analysis skill,

198
00:10:37.003 --> 00:10:39.872
and a data visualization code gen skill.

199
00:10:39.872 --> 00:10:43.342
I invite you to pause the video here
and think of a few different evaluations

200
00:10:43.342 --> 00:10:46.379
that can be used to evaluate
each of these three different skills,

201
00:10:46.979 --> 00:10:50.683
whether the entire skill
or one single part of the skill. As

202
00:10:50.683 --> 00:10:53.853
in the case of this lookup sales
data tool, you have a step

203
00:10:53.853 --> 00:10:57.123
to prepare the database,
generate SQL, execute SQL.

204
00:10:57.590 --> 00:11:00.793
So you might have evaluations
on the full lookup sales data tool,

205
00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:03.863
or on one step of that process.

206
00:11:03.863 --> 00:11:07.033
So here are a set of evaluators
that you could use to evaluate

207
00:11:07.033 --> 00:11:08.601
each of those different tools.

208
00:11:08.601 --> 00:11:12.271
So for the database lookup tool
you could use either LLM

209
00:11:12.271 --> 00:11:16.208
as-a-Judge or a code-based eval
to do SQL generation correctness

210
00:11:16.208 --> 00:11:20.646
and see if the SQL that's generated
is correct. For the data analysis tool,

211
00:11:20.713 --> 00:11:24.583
you could use an LLM as-a-Judge
to check for clarity in the analysis,

212
00:11:24.850 --> 00:11:27.620
and make sure all of the entities
that are mentioned in that analysis

213
00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:28.587
are correct and match

214
00:11:28.587 --> 00:11:32.024
back to entities in the input
or other sections of their data.

215
00:11:32.858 --> 00:11:35.194
And for the data visualization
code gen tool,

216
00:11:35.194 --> 00:11:38.764
you can use a code-based eval to make sure
the code is runnable.

217
00:11:39.198 --> 00:11:41.934
Fairly straightforward eval
that you can run there.

218
00:11:41.934 --> 00:11:45.004
Now, it's important to mention
that you could have come up with different

219
00:11:45.004 --> 00:11:46.272
evals to use here.

220
00:11:46.272 --> 00:11:49.542
And again, eval is sometimes
more of an art than a science.

221
00:11:49.942 --> 00:11:53.079
And so if you came up with different ideas
don't be discouraged.

222
00:11:53.112 --> 00:11:55.881
Those could be just as correct
or in some cases more correct here too.

223
00:11:56.882 --> 00:11:59.518
The last thing
to mention is that you have SQL generation

224
00:11:59.518 --> 00:12:03.222
correctness being run with either LLM
as-a-Judge or code-based evals.

225
00:12:03.522 --> 00:12:05.891
You'll see what both
those look like in future notebooks.

226
00:12:05.891 --> 00:12:09.462
You can either use an LLM
as-a-Judge to judge whether this equals

227
00:12:09.528 --> 00:12:13.165
correctly generated, or you can use a code-based
eval to compare

228
00:12:13.399 --> 00:12:16.168
